export const SECTIONS = [
  {
    name: "Domain 1 — Coding Knowledge and Skills",
    sourceNote: SOURCE_NOTE_D1,
    questions: [
      { stem: "Per UHDDS, the principal diagnosis is defined as:", options: ["The diagnosis that required the most resource utilization during the stay", "The condition established after study to be chiefly responsible for occasioning the admission of the patient to the hospital for care", "The first diagnosis listed by the attending physician on the face sheet", "Any condition that co-existed at admission and affected length of stay"], correct: 1, explanation: "UHDDS defines the principal diagnosis as the condition established AFTER STUDY to be chiefly responsible for occasioning the admission — not simply the first-listed condition or the most resource-intensive one." },
      { stem: "A patient is admitted with acute exacerbation of COPD and is also found to have an incidental, asymptomatic 4mm pulmonary nodule requiring no treatment during this stay. Which coding principle applies to the nodule?", options: ["It must always be coded, since ICD-10-CM requires reporting every documented condition regardless of clinical significance", "It should be coded only if it meets the definition of an additional/secondary diagnosis — one that requires clinical evaluation, therapeutic treatment, diagnostic procedures, extended LOS, or increased nursing/monitoring", "It should never be coded because it was incidental", "It should be coded as the principal diagnosis because it was discovered during this admission"], correct: 1, explanation: "The Official Guidelines' definition of 'additional diagnosis' — not blanket 'code everything documented' — governs whether a secondary condition is reportable; a truly incidental finding with no associated clinical significance/evaluation would not meet that definition." },
      { stem: "In ICD-10-PCS, the 3rd character of a Medical and Surgical section code represents:", options: ["Body System", "Root Operation", "Approach", "Body Part"], correct: 1, explanation: "ICD-10-PCS Medical and Surgical section structure: 1=Section, 2=Body System, 3=Root Operation, 4=Body Part, 5=Approach, 6=Device, 7=Qualifier." },
      { stem: "Which ICD-10-PCS root operation is defined as 'cutting out or off, without replacement, a portion of a body part'?", options: ["Resection", "Excision", "Extraction", "Destruction"], correct: 1, explanation: "Excision = cutting out/off a PORTION of a body part without replacement. Resection is the same definition but for the ENTIRE body part. Extraction is pulling/stripping out. Destruction is eradicating without taking any of it out." },
      { stem: "Which ICD-10-PCS root operation is defined as 'cutting out or off, without replacement, ALL of a body part'?", options: ["Excision", "Resection", "Detachment", "Fragmentation"], correct: 1, explanation: "Resection removes the entire body part; Excision removes only a portion — this distinction (partial vs. entire) is one of the most heavily tested root-operation pairs on the CCS exam." },
      { stem: "A patient undergoes a procedure to restore, to the extent possible, a body part to its normal anatomic structure and function after it was damaged by disease or trauma. Which ICD-10-PCS root operation applies?", options: ["Repair", "Reposition", "Restriction", "Supplement"], correct: 0, explanation: "Repair is the 'not elsewhere classified' root operation for restoring anatomic structure/function using methods other than the other specifically-defined root operations." },
      { stem: "Present on Admission (POA) indicator 'W' means:", options: ["The condition was clinically undetermined — the provider could not determine whether it was present on admission", "The condition was not present on admission", "The condition was present on admission", "The diagnosis is exempt from POA reporting"], correct: 0, explanation: "POA indicators: Y=present on admission, N=not present on admission, U=documentation insufficient to determine, W=clinically undetermined (provider unable to determine whether present on admission), 1=exempt from POA reporting." },
      { stem: "A condition is documented as 'possible' pneumonia at the time of discharge from an inpatient stay, with no more definitive diagnosis ever established. Per the Official Guidelines for inpatient coding, this should be:", options: ["Never coded, since it was never confirmed", "Coded as if it existed, because the inpatient guideline for uncertain diagnoses ('probable,' 'suspected,' 'likely,' 'questionable,' 'possible,' 'still to be ruled out') directs coding the condition as if it were established", "Queried as mandatory before any code can be assigned", "Coded only as a symptom, never the named condition"], correct: 1, explanation: "The inpatient-only 'uncertain diagnosis' guideline allows coding a possible/probable/suspected condition documented at discharge as if confirmed — this guideline does NOT apply to outpatient coding, which is a frequently tested contrast." },
      { stem: "Which of the following is the correct treatment of an uncertain diagnosis ('rule out appendicitis') in the OUTPATIENT setting, per ICD-10-CM guidelines?", options: ["Code the condition (appendicitis) as if confirmed, same as inpatient", "Do not code the uncertain condition — code only the documented signs/symptoms prompting the visit", "Code both the uncertain condition and the symptoms together as co-principal", "Query the provider before the encounter can be coded at all"], correct: 1, explanation: "Outpatient/ambulatory guidelines are the opposite of the inpatient uncertain-diagnosis rule: do not code conditions described as probable/suspected/rule-out — code to the highest degree of certainty, i.e., the signs and symptoms." },
      { stem: "A Major Complication/Comorbidity (MCC) or Complication/Comorbidity (CC), when accurately captured through coding, primarily affects:", options: ["The patient's discharge disposition code only", "MS-DRG assignment and associated reimbursement weight", "The sequence of the principal diagnosis only, never reimbursement", "Only the POA indicator, not the DRG"], correct: 1, explanation: "CC/MCC capture is the mechanism by which MS-DRG assignment reflects severity/resource intensity — an MCC generally moves a case into a higher-weighted DRG tier than a CC, and no CC/MCC into the base DRG." },
      { stem: "For a patient with diabetes mellitus and a documented diabetic condition (e.g., diabetic nephropathy), current ICD-10-CM guidelines require:", options: ["Coding diabetes and the associated condition as two entirely unrelated, separately sequenced diagnoses with no combination logic", "Using the combination-code structure in the diabetes mellitus codes, which link diabetes with its documented associated manifestation using 'with' in the Alphabetic Index", "Coding only the manifestation, never diabetes itself", "Always sequencing the manifestation before diabetes"], correct: 1, explanation: "ICD-10-CM's diabetes codes are combination codes; per the guidelines, the word 'with' in the Alphabetic Index should be interpreted as a link between diabetes and the listed condition even without a physician explicitly stating a causal relationship, unless the documentation states the conditions are unrelated." },
      { stem: "A patient is admitted for surgical treatment of a condition, but during the same admission develops an unrelated acute condition requiring equal or more intensive care. Under UHDDS principal-diagnosis selection rules, when two or more conditions meet the definition for principal diagnosis, coding guidance directs:", options: ["Sequence in alphabetical order of the diagnosis descriptions", "Any one of them may be sequenced first, unless the circumstances of admission, diagnostic workup, and/or therapy provided indicate otherwise", "Always sequence the surgical condition first", "Sequence based on which condition was documented earliest in the chart"], correct: 1, explanation: "When two or more diagnoses equally meet the definition of principal diagnosis, the guidelines allow sequencing either first UNLESS the circumstances of the admission, diagnostic workup, and/or therapy indicate a different principal diagnosis is more appropriate." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best describes the ICD-10-PCS root operation 'Control'?", options: ["Stopping, or attempting to stop, postprocedural bleeding", "Taking out solid matter from a body part", "Putting in a nonbiological device that monitors physiological function", "Moving a body part to a different location"], correct: 0, explanation: "Control is specifically for stopping/attempting to stop post-procedural bleeding, distinct from Repair (which would be used if control of bleeding required more than simple techniques, e.g., suturing a bleeding vessel)." },
      { stem: "A patient has a laparoscopic cholecystectomy that is converted to an open procedure due to complications. Per ICD-10-PCS coding conventions for converted procedures, the approach coded is:", options: ["The laparoscopic (percutaneous endoscopic) approach only, since that's how it started", "The approach actually completed — the open approach — is the one coded, not the approach that was attempted and abandoned", "Both approaches are coded as separate procedures", "Neither approach is coded; only the body part is coded"], correct: 1, explanation: "When an approach is converted, ICD-10-PCS conventions direct coding the approach that was ultimately used to complete the procedure, not an approach that was attempted and converted away from." },
      { stem: "Which root operation involves 'putting in a device that monitors, assists, performs, or prevents a physiological function but does not physically take the place of a body part'?", options: ["Replacement", "Insertion", "Supplement", "Change"], correct: 1, explanation: "Insertion = putting in a nonbiological device that monitors/assists/performs/prevents a function without replacing a body part (e.g., pacemaker lead). Replacement physically takes the place of a body part; Supplement reinforces/augments a body part with a device or autologous/nonautologous material." },
      { stem: "A hospital's coding staff must determine whether a diagnosis is an 'other/secondary diagnosis' for UHDDS reporting purposes. Which of the following would NOT independently qualify a condition as a reportable secondary diagnosis?", options: ["It required clinical evaluation during the stay", "It required an extended length of hospital stay", "It was mentioned once in the history section with no further workup, treatment, monitoring, or impact on the stay", "It required increased nursing care or monitoring"], correct: 2, explanation: "A condition that is merely documented in history with zero clinical evaluation, treatment, extended stay, or monitoring impact does not meet UHDDS's 'additional diagnosis' definition and should not automatically be coded as a secondary diagnosis." },
      { stem: "For a two-stage bilateral joint replacement performed during the same operative episode on both the right and left knee, ICD-10-PCS coding convention requires:", options: ["One combined code representing both knees", "A separate procedure code for each knee, since each is a distinct body part value even though the root operation and device are identical", "Only the right side is coded; laterality is assumed", "The bilateral body part value 'Z' is always used regardless of whether ICD-10-PCS defines distinct laterality values"], correct: 1, explanation: "ICD-10-PCS body part values are generally side-specific (right/left); a bilateral procedure on paired body parts each having distinct body part values requires a separate procedure code for each side, per the multiple-procedures coding convention." },
      { stem: "In ICD-10-PCS, which root operation is defined as 'taking out solid matter from a body part'?", options: ["Extraction", "Excision", "Drainage", "Fragmentation"], correct: 0, explanation: "Extraction = pulling or stripping out (by force) all or a portion of a body part, using the force of the extraction to separate the portion being taken out (e.g., D&C, vein stripping, tooth extraction)." },
      { stem: "Which ICD-10-PCS root operation means 'taking out or eliminating all or a portion of a body part by the use of force to separate or cut off the body part' via extreme cold, chemicals, or radiofrequency energy rather than mechanical means?", options: ["Fragmentation", "Destruction", "Excision", "Resection"], correct: 1, explanation: "Destruction physically eradicates body part tissue by direct application of energy (e.g., cryoablation, radiofrequency ablation, fulguration) with no part of the body part being physically taken out — distinguishing it from Excision/Resection." },
      { stem: "According to the Official Guidelines, when a patient is admitted for a condition and, after study, that condition is determined not to require further care, but a comorbidity discovered during the stay ends up requiring the majority of the workup and treatment, the principal diagnosis is:", options: ["Always the comorbidity that received the most treatment", "Determined per UHDDS by the condition established after study to have occasioned the admission — not simply which condition received the most resource use during the stay", "Whichever was coded first in the chart", "Split evenly as a co-principal diagnosis by default"], correct: 1, explanation: "Resource intensity during the stay is not the UHDDS test for principal diagnosis — the reason the admission itself was occasioned, established after study, governs, even if a different condition consumed more care once hospitalized." },
      { stem: "A 'combination code' in ICD-10-CM is best described as:", options: ["A single code used to classify two diagnoses, or a diagnosis with an associated secondary process/manifestation, or a diagnosis with an associated complication", "Any code that requires two providers to co-sign", "A code that must always be reported with a companion HCPCS code", "A placeholder code awaiting further diagnostic workup"], correct: 0, explanation: "Combination codes classify two diagnoses together, a diagnosis with an associated manifestation, or a diagnosis with an associated complication, all within one code — common in conditions like diabetes with manifestations." },
      { stem: "Which ICD-10-PCS root operation is defined as 'moving to its normal location, or other suitable location, all or a portion of a body part'?", options: ["Reposition", "Transfer", "Reattachment", "Restriction"], correct: 0, explanation: "Reposition moves a body part to a normal or other suitable location without taking out any of the body part (e.g., reducing a fracture, repositioning an undescended testicle)." },
      { stem: "A patient is coded with a 7th character extension of 'S' on an injury code. What does this extension represent?", options: ["Subsequent encounter", "Sequela", "Initial encounter, severe", "Same-day surgery"], correct: 1, explanation: "For injury, poisoning, and certain other codes requiring 7th character extensions: A=initial encounter, D=subsequent encounter, S=sequela (a condition that is the residual effect after the acute phase of an illness/injury has ended)." },
      { stem: "The 'excludes1' note in ICD-10-CM means:", options: ["The excluded condition may be coded together with the code above it when both are documented", "A pure code-also note requiring both codes always", "The excluded condition is NOT coded here — the two conditions cannot occur together (e.g., a congenital form vs. an acquired form of the same condition)", "The excluded condition should be coded first, then the code with the note"], correct: 2, explanation: "Excludes1 = 'not coded here' — the two conditions cannot be reported together because they aren't clinically possible to coexist as classified (e.g., a code and its congenital version). Excludes2 means 'not included here' — the excluded condition IS permitted to be coded together with the other." },
      { stem: "For DRG-based inpatient reimbursement, which of the following most directly determines whether a case groups to a higher-weighted MS-DRG within the same base DRG family?", options: ["The total number of ICD-10-CM codes assigned, regardless of severity", "Documentation and coding of a qualifying CC or MCC as a secondary diagnosis", "The length of the discharge summary", "The number of consulting physicians involved"], correct: 1, explanation: "MS-DRGs are typically structured in three severity tiers per base DRG (with MCC, with CC, without CC/MCC) — accurately capturing a qualifying CC/MCC secondary diagnosis is what moves a case to a higher-weighted tier, not raw code count or documentation length." },
      { stem: "Which ICD-10-PCS root operation describes 'putting back in, or taking out and putting back in, all or a portion of a separated body part to its normal location or other suitable location'?", options: ["Reattachment", "Reposition", "Restoration", "Revision"], correct: 0, explanation: "Reattachment reconnects a separated body part (e.g., a severed finger) — distinct from Reposition, which moves a body part that was never separated from the body." },
      { stem: "A patient's ICD-10-PCS procedure involves correcting, to the extent possible, a portion of a malfunctioning device without taking out and replacing the entire device. Which root operation applies?", options: ["Revision", "Removal", "Change", "Replacement"], correct: 0, explanation: "Revision corrects, to the extent possible, a malfunctioning or displaced device — Removal takes a device out with no replacement, Change exchanges a device without cutting/puncturing, and Replacement physically substitutes a body part." },
      { stem: "In ICD-10-CM sequencing, when a patient is admitted specifically for the management of a malignancy (e.g., chemotherapy) and the treatment is the sole reason for admission, the principal diagnosis is typically:", options: ["The malignancy itself, always, regardless of the reason for the encounter", "The appropriate code for the treatment encounter (e.g., encounter for chemotherapy), with the malignancy sequenced as a secondary diagnosis, per the neoplasm coding guideline", "Never codeable without a query", "The malignancy's histology code only"], correct: 1, explanation: "Per the ICD-10-CM neoplasm guidelines, when the encounter is solely for administration of chemotherapy/immunotherapy/radiation therapy, the appropriate Z code for the encounter is sequenced first, with the malignancy coded as an additional diagnosis." },
      { stem: "The term 'laterality' in ICD-10-CM/PCS refers to:", options: ["Whether a condition is chronic or acute", "The specification of right, left, or bilateral involvement of a paired organ or body part", "The severity level of a disease", "The timing of onset relative to admission"], correct: 1, explanation: "Laterality specifies right/left/bilateral for paired organs and body parts, and unspecified-laterality codes should only be used when the medical record genuinely does not document laterality — not as a default convenience." },
      { stem: "A discharge summary documents 'sepsis due to E. coli UTI, resulting in severe sepsis with acute kidney injury.' Per the sepsis coding guidelines, the coding of severe sepsis requires:", options: ["A single code covering the entire clinical picture", "The underlying systemic infection code sequenced first, followed by a code for severe sepsis (R65.2-), followed by codes for the specific acute organ dysfunction(s)", "Only the acute kidney injury code, since it is the most acute finding", "Only a code for sepsis, with organ dysfunction never separately coded"], correct: 1, explanation: "Severe sepsis coding requires (at minimum) the underlying infection code, a code from the R65.2- severe sepsis subcategory, and code(s) for the specific associated acute organ dysfunction(s) — omitting the organ dysfunction code is a common real-world coding error." },
      { stem: "Which ICD-10-PCS root operation means 'altering the natural anatomic structure of a body part without taking out or replacing any of the body part, to restrict or narrow the diameter/orifice'?", options: ["Restriction", "Occlusion", "Dilation", "Constriction"], correct: 0, explanation: "Restriction partially narrows the diameter/orifice of a tubular body part (e.g., gastric banding); Occlusion completely closes it off; Dilation expands/widens it." },
      { stem: "Which of the following correctly distinguishes 'Occlusion' from 'Restriction' in ICD-10-PCS?", options: ["Occlusion completely closes an orifice or lumen; Restriction only partially narrows it", "They are interchangeable terms for the same root operation", "Occlusion always requires a device; Restriction never does", "Restriction is used only on the cardiovascular system"], correct: 0, explanation: "Occlusion completely closes an orifice/lumen (e.g., clipping an aneurysm neck completely); Restriction only partially narrows it (e.g., a restrictive band). Both may or may not use a device." },
      { stem: "A coder assigns codes for an encounter documenting 'chronic kidney disease, stage 3, due to hypertension.' ICD-10-CM's hypertensive chronic kidney disease combination code assumes a causal relationship between hypertension and CKD:", options: ["Only if the physician explicitly writes 'due to' or 'secondary to'", "Automatically, per the classification's presumed causal relationship, unless the documentation clearly states the conditions are unrelated", "Never — the two must always be linked explicitly by the provider", "Only in the outpatient setting"], correct: 1, explanation: "Per ICD-10-CM guidelines, a causal relationship between hypertension and CKD is presumed by classification, and both conditions are coded as related even without explicit provider linkage — unless documentation clearly indicates they are unrelated." },
      { stem: "Which ICD-10-PCS root operation is defined as 'putting in a biological or synthetic material that physically takes the place of all or a portion of a body part'?", options: ["Supplement", "Replacement", "Insertion", "Transplantation"], correct: 1, explanation: "Replacement physically takes the place of a body part (autograft, allograft, or synthetic substitute, e.g., total knee arthroplasty) — the original body part may or may not be physically taken out." },
      { stem: "A malignant neoplasm has metastasized from its primary site to a secondary site. When both the primary and secondary sites require treatment during an encounter, and the reason for the encounter is treatment of the metastatic (secondary) site, sequencing is:", options: ["Primary site is always sequenced first", "The secondary (metastatic) site is sequenced as principal/first-listed when treatment is directed at the secondary site, per the neoplasm guideline", "Only the primary site is ever coded once metastasis occurs", "Sequencing is determined solely by which site was diagnosed first, chronologically"], correct: 1, explanation: "The neoplasm coding guideline directs sequencing based on which site (primary or secondary) is the reason for the current encounter/treatment focus, not automatically defaulting to the primary site." },
      { stem: "A postoperative complication code should be assigned only when:", options: ["Any adverse outcome occurs after any procedure, regardless of physician documentation", "The provider has documented a relationship between the condition and the procedure/care — a coder cannot assume a cause-and-effect relationship between a complication and surgical/medical care", "The patient was on the operating table for more than 4 hours", "It occurs strictly within 24 hours of the procedure"], correct: 1, explanation: "Coding a condition as a postprocedural/postoperative complication requires explicit provider documentation establishing that relationship — coders may not infer a cause-and-effect link from timing or proximity alone." },
      { stem: "Which of the following ICD-10-PCS Medical and Surgical root operations always involves taking out all of a body part?", options: ["Excision", "Resection", "Extirpation", "Fragmentation"], correct: 1, explanation: "Resection is defined as cutting out/off, without replacement, ALL of a body part — the defining feature distinguishing it from Excision (a portion)." },
      { stem: "In the inpatient setting, if a patient's final diagnosis at discharge includes a condition documented only as 'suspected,' and the coder is uncertain whether it should be coded as confirmed, the most appropriate first action per AHIMA's Standards of Ethical Coding is:", options: ["Code it as confirmed automatically, since the inpatient uncertain-diagnosis guideline always applies without exception", "Never code it and drop it from the record", "Query the provider for clarification when the documentation is ambiguous rather than assume, consistent with ethical coding standards that prohibit coding based on assumption when clarification is reasonably obtainable", "Assign the least specific code available and move on"], correct: 2, explanation: "While the inpatient uncertain-diagnosis guideline does permit coding 'possible/probable/suspected' conditions as if confirmed, ethical coding practice still calls for querying when documentation is genuinely ambiguous or when the coder cannot confidently apply the guideline to the specific clinical picture — coding should never be based on assumption when a compliant query is a reasonable option." },
      { stem: "A 'Z code' in ICD-10-CM is generally used to report:", options: ["Only poisoning and adverse effects of drugs", "Encounters for reasons other than a current illness/injury, such as factors influencing health status, aftercare, screening, or history of a condition", "Only injuries from external causes", "Only laboratory abnormalities with no clinical significance"], correct: 1, explanation: "Z codes (Factors Influencing Health Status and Contact with Health Services) cover encounters not primarily driven by acute illness/injury — health maintenance visits, screening, aftercare, personal/family history, and similar circumstances." },
      { stem: "A patient is admitted through the Emergency Department in septic shock secondary to a urinary tract infection. Per the sepsis sequencing guideline, the appropriate principal diagnosis is generally:", options: ["The UTI code alone, since it is the origin of the infection", "The underlying systemic infection (sepsis due to the UTI), sequenced first, followed by the code for severe sepsis and then septic shock, per the sepsis guideline's sequencing hierarchy", "Septic shock alone, since it is the most acute condition", "The UTI and sepsis coded as unrelated, co-equal principal diagnoses"], correct: 1, explanation: "Sepsis sequencing requires the underlying systemic infection first, followed by the R65.2- severe sepsis code, with septic shock (a form of acute organ dysfunction) coded as well — septic shock is never sequenced as principal, and shock is always coded in addition to, not instead of, the underlying sepsis/infection." },
    ],
  },
  {
    name: "Domain 2 — Coding Documentation",
    sourceNote: SOURCE_NOTE_D2,
    questions: [
      { stem: "A health record documents 'right leg' in the admission history but 'left leg' in the operative report for the same procedure/injury. This is an example of:", options: ["Acceptable documentation variance requiring no action", "Conflicting documentation that must be resolved before a laterality-specific code can be confidently assigned — typically via a provider query", "A coder discretion issue where the coder should simply pick the more recent note", "Something that should be resolved by defaulting to 'bilateral, unspecified'"], correct: 1, explanation: "Genuinely conflicting documentation about a clinically significant detail like laterality cannot simply be guessed at or defaulted — it should be resolved, typically through querying the responsible provider(s), before finalizing the code." },
      { stem: "For a code to be validly assigned, required supporting documentation must generally be located:", options: ["Anywhere in the patient's lifetime medical history across all facilities", "Within the body of the health record for the encounter being coded, documented by a provider who is legally accountable for establishing the patient's diagnosis", "In the discharge summary exclusively, never elsewhere in the chart", "In the nursing notes exclusively"], correct: 1, explanation: "Codeable diagnoses generally must be documented by a provider legally responsible for the diagnosis/care, within the health record for that encounter — a nurse's or ancillary staff note alone typically cannot establish a new diagnosis for coding purposes." },
      { stem: "Which of the following is the best example of 'verifying and validating documentation' before finalizing a code assignment?", options: ["Assuming a diagnosis is accurate simply because it appears in the coding summary sheet without cross-checking the record", "Cross-referencing a diagnosis or procedure documented in one part of the record (e.g., the problem list) against supporting clinical evidence elsewhere in the record (labs, orders, provider notes) to confirm consistency", "Coding directly from a diagnosis-related billing sheet prepared by non-clinical administrative staff with no chart review at all", "Relying solely on the discharge summary's ICD-10 code list without reading any clinical notes"], correct: 1, explanation: "Validating documentation means confirming that a diagnosis/procedure is consistently and adequately supported across the record — not coding mechanically from a summary sheet or list without underlying clinical corroboration." },
      { stem: "A physician documents 'CHF' in a progress note but the type (systolic, diastolic, acute, chronic) is never specified anywhere else in the record. For accurate, specific ICD-10-CM code assignment, the coder should:", options: ["Assign the most common subtype code as a default assumption", "Recognize that unspecified documentation limits the coder to an unspecified heart failure code, or query the provider if a more specific code is likely warranted and obtainable", "Never code heart failure at all", "Code both systolic and diastolic heart failure to cover all possibilities"], correct: 1, explanation: "Coders cannot infer clinical specificity that isn't documented — the choice is either an unspecified code (if no further information is obtainable) or a compliant query to the provider for clarification, never guessing a specific subtype." },
      { stem: "Which of the following scenarios represents a documentation completeness issue that would prevent confident ICD-10-PCS procedure code assignment?", options: ["The operative report clearly documents the approach, body part, and root operation performed", "The operative report is missing entirely, and only a preoperative order for 'possible appendectomy' exists in the chart, with no documentation of what was actually performed", "The pathology report confirms a diagnosis already suspected preoperatively", "The anesthesia record documents the type of anesthesia used"], correct: 1, explanation: "Without an operative report or equivalent documentation of what was actually done, a coder has no valid basis to assign a specific procedure code — a preoperative order describing an intended/possible procedure is not evidence of what was actually performed." },
      { stem: "A discrepancy exists between the admission type recorded in the registration system (elective) and clinical documentation suggesting the patient was actually admitted emergently from the ED. This type of conflict falls under which documentation-integrity task?", options: ["Provider query compliance", "Resolving conflicting documentation in the health record", "HITECH compliance", "Encoder software configuration"], correct: 1, explanation: "This is a direct example of 'resolve conflicting documentation in the health record (e.g., admission type, laterality)' — one of the explicitly listed CCS Domain 2 tasks." },
      { stem: "Verifying that documentation supports a reported present-on-admission indicator most directly relies on:", options: ["The billing department's internal notes", "Clinical documentation clearly establishing the timing of onset relative to the inpatient admission", "The patient's verbal report to registration staff only", "The length of stay alone"], correct: 1, explanation: "POA determination is a clinical documentation question — whether the condition was present at the time of the order for inpatient admission — and requires clear clinical documentation of onset timing, not administrative or patient-reported data alone." },
      { stem: "A coder notices that a diagnosis appears on the coding summary but nowhere else in the actual health record narrative, labs, orders, or notes. The appropriate action is:", options: ["Code it anyway since it's on the summary sheet", "Do not assign a code based solely on a summary sheet entry unsupported by documentation elsewhere in the record — verify against the full record or query", "Delete the summary sheet", "Assign the code but flag it as 'unconfirmed' in the billing system, without further follow-up"], correct: 1, explanation: "Coding must be based on documentation within the health record itself, not solely a coding/charge summary sheet — an unsupported entry should prompt verification against the full record or a compliant query, not automatic code assignment." },
      { stem: "In a scenario where two attending physicians document different, non-conflicting but individually incomplete pieces of the clinical picture (e.g., one documents the diagnosis, another documents the treatment plan for it), the coder should:", options: ["Only use documentation from the most senior physician", "Synthesize accurate coding from the complete record across all legitimate provider documentation, since UHDDS reporting is based on the record as a whole, not a single provider's note in isolation", "Query both physicians regardless of whether the picture is already clear from combined documentation", "Refuse to code until a single physician documents the entire picture in one note"], correct: 1, explanation: "Coding is based on the health record as a whole; complementary (non-conflicting) documentation from multiple legitimate providers can together support accurate code assignment without requiring one single all-encompassing note." },
      { stem: "Which best describes 'required documentation elements' for assigning a diagnosis code with a comorbidity/complication (CC/MCC) designation?", options: ["No specific documentation standard applies to CC/MCC-designated diagnoses", "The condition must be clearly and consistently documented as clinically significant/being treated, monitored, or evaluated during the stay — not merely listed once without clinical substantiation", "Any mention anywhere in the chart is automatically sufficient regardless of clinical substantiation", "CC/MCC diagnoses require documentation only in the discharge summary"], correct: 1, explanation: "Because CC/MCC-designated diagnoses can materially affect DRG assignment and reimbursement, they carry heightened documentation-integrity expectations — the condition needs credible clinical support (evaluation, treatment, monitoring) consistent with UHDDS's additional-diagnosis definition, not a single unsupported mention." },
      { stem: "A clinical documentation improvement (CDI) specialist flags a record where the H&P states 'malnutrition' but there is no documented BMI, weight loss percentage, or nutritional assessment supporting a specific severity of malnutrition. From a documentation-integrity standpoint, this is:", options: ["A non-issue; any mention of malnutrition supports the most severe code available", "A gap requiring either additional supporting clinical indicators in the record or a compliant query to establish/confirm the severity before assigning a specific severity code", "Something the CDI specialist alone can resolve by picking the code without coder or provider involvement", "Irrelevant to DRG assignment"], correct: 1, explanation: "Severity-specific diagnoses like malnutrition staging require supporting clinical indicators consistent with recognized criteria; absent that support, the gap should be closed via additional documentation or a compliant query, not defaulted to the most severe available code." },
      { stem: "Which of the following is an example of appropriately 'ensuring all required documentation for assigning a specified code is available within the body of the health record'?", options: ["Confirming a secondary diagnosis coded for CC/MCC purposes has documented evidence of clinical evaluation, treatment, or monitoring somewhere in the actual chart, not just an entry on an unsupported list", "Assuming documentation exists because the diagnosis appears plausible clinically", "Skipping verification for diagnoses that don't affect DRG weight", "Verifying only diagnoses explicitly requested by the billing department"], correct: 0, explanation: "This task is specifically about confirming that supporting documentation actually exists in the record for any code being assigned — most critically important, though not exclusively, for diagnoses that affect DRG/severity weighting." },
      { stem: "A discharge summary states a patient has 'history of MI' with no date given, while a cardiology consult note from the same admission documents an 'acute STEMI' actively being treated during this stay. The correct interpretation is:", options: ["These are the same event and only the discharge summary's 'history of MI' should be coded, as a personal history code", "These likely represent two different clinical facts (an old, resolved MI in history, and a new acute MI this admission) requiring careful review — not automatic collapsing into one code — and a query if the timeline is genuinely unclear", "The discrepancy is a HIPAA violation requiring reporting", "The two notes must always be reconciled by deleting one of them from the chart"], correct: 1, explanation: "This is a documentation-consistency scenario: it's plausible both are true and non-conflicting (old MI in history + a new acute MI this admission), but the coder must review carefully — collapsing them incorrectly, or ignoring a genuine ambiguity, both risk an inaccurate code assignment." },
      { stem: "Documentation validation for procedure coding should confirm that the operative report is:", options: ["Co-signed by a family member", "Authenticated (signed) by the performing provider and consistent with other record entries (anesthesia record, pathology, nursing notes) describing the same episode of care", "Written exclusively in template/checkbox format with no free text", "Filed within 30 days of discharge, with no other requirement"], correct: 1, explanation: "Valid procedure documentation should be properly authenticated by the performing provider and internally consistent with other documentation of the same episode — inconsistency across these sources is a red flag requiring further review." },
      { stem: "Which scenario best illustrates a documentation conflict that specifically affects POA determination (not just diagnosis specificity)?", options: ["A wound is documented as 'stage 2 pressure ulcer' by wound care on day 3 of the stay, with no ED or admission documentation addressing skin integument at all, making it unclear whether it existed at admission", "A lab value is slightly abnormal but clinically insignificant", "A medication list is missing a dosage strength", "A discharge instruction sheet has a typo in the follow-up date"], correct: 0, explanation: "Without any admission-time documentation of skin condition, whether a pressure ulcer identified later was present on admission is genuinely unclear — exactly the kind of conflict/gap that affects POA assignment and often requires a query or review of nursing admission assessments." },
      { stem: "A coder is assigning a procedure code from an operative report that describes the surgeon converting from a planned laparoscopic approach to open, but the report never explicitly restates the final approach in its closing summary line. The correct documentation-review practice is:", options: ["Assume the laparoscopic approach was used because that's what was originally planned", "Read the full body of the operative note narrative (not just a summary line) to determine the approach actually completed, since ICD-10-PCS coding is based on what was actually performed, not what was planned or a possibly-incomplete summary line", "Query the surgeon before reading the rest of the note", "Default to 'open' any time conversion is mentioned anywhere, without reading further"], correct: 1, explanation: "Coders must review the full narrative of an operative report, not rely on a potentially incomplete summary line, to accurately determine what approach and technique were actually completed — this is a core documentation-review competency, distinct from simply defaulting based on partial information." },
      { stem: "A nursing intake assessment documents a decubitus ulcer at admission, but no physician note anywhere in the record mentions it, and no treatment/monitoring for it is documented by any provider. From a coding-documentation standpoint, this is best handled by:", options: ["Coding it automatically from the nursing note alone, since nursing documentation is always sufficient to establish a diagnosis for coding", "Recognizing that most diagnoses require provider (not nursing-alone) documentation to be codeable, and either locating physician documentation elsewhere in the record or querying the provider to confirm/establish the diagnosis, consistent with the general principle that a diagnosis must be documented by someone legally accountable for it", "Ignoring the nursing note entirely with no further action", "Coding it as present on admission automatically without provider confirmation of the diagnosis itself"], correct: 1, explanation: "While nursing documentation (e.g., wound assessments) is a valid input for clinical facts like staging or POA determination once a diagnosis exists, the diagnosis itself generally still needs physician/qualified-provider documentation to be codeable — an isolated nursing note with no physician corroboration is a documentation gap to close via query or further chart review." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best reflects proper handling of an amended or corrected note in the health record for coding purposes?", options: ["Coders should always use the original, unamended version of a note, ignoring any later corrections", "Coders should use the most current, properly authenticated version of the documentation (including compliant amendments/addenda), since the record's amendment/correction process is designed to reflect the accurate clinical picture", "Amended notes can never be used for coding under any circumstances", "Only amendments made within 24 hours of the original note are valid for coding"], correct: 1, explanation: "Proper amendments/addenda, made following an organization's compliant correction process, become part of the legal health record and should be used for coding — the goal is coding from the most accurate, current documentation, not artificially freezing on an outdated or erroneous original entry." },
      { stem: "A discharge summary and an ED physician note disagree on whether a patient's altered mental status was due to hypoglycemia or a new stroke, with no later note in the record resolving which was accurate. This scenario requires:", options: ["The coder picking whichever diagnosis appears first chronologically in the chart", "Recognizing this as unresolved conflicting documentation regarding the actual clinical diagnosis — appropriately addressed through further chart review for a resolving note or a compliant, non-leading query to the responsible provider(s), not an unsupported coder guess", "Coding both conditions as co-principal automatically with no further review", "Defaulting to the ED note only, since it was documented first"], correct: 1, explanation: "Truly unresolved clinical disagreement about the actual diagnosis (not just a wording or administrative discrepancy) is a documentation-integrity issue requiring resolution — via further record review or a compliant query — rather than an arbitrary coder choice between conflicting provider opinions." },
      { stem: "A coder abstracting data for UHDDS reporting notices that the documented discharge disposition (e.g., 'discharged to home' vs. 'discharged to skilled nursing facility') differs between the case management note and the physician's discharge order. Because discharge disposition affects both UHDDS reporting and MS-DRG post-acute transfer payment policy, the coder should:", options: ["Ignore the discrepancy since disposition doesn't affect anything coding-related", "Resolve the discrepancy against the health record before finalizing abstracted data, since an inaccurate disposition can both misstate UHDDS data and trigger an incorrect post-acute-transfer payment adjustment", "Always default to whichever disposition results in higher reimbursement", "Report both dispositions simultaneously on the claim"], correct: 1, explanation: "Discharge disposition is a UHDDS data element with direct payment consequences (post-acute-care transfer policy can reduce payment for certain MS-DRGs when disposition is to another facility rather than home) — a genuine conflict here must be resolved from the record, not assumed." },
    ],
  },
  {
    name: "Domain 3 — Provider Queries",
    sourceNote: SOURCE_NOTE_D3,
    questions: [
      { stem: "Which of the following is a core element of an ethical, compliant provider query?", options: ["It suggests only one specific diagnosis the coder believes is correct, worded so the provider need only agree", "It is non-leading — presenting clinical indicators and offering multiple reasonable clinical options (including 'other,' 'clinically undetermined,' or 'not clinically significant') rather than suggesting a predetermined answer", "It is submitted verbally only, with no written or auditable trail", "It is generated automatically by encoder software without any clinical indicator review"], correct: 1, explanation: "A compliant query must be non-leading: it presents relevant clinical indicators and reasonable, clinically plausible options without suggesting a specific diagnosis the provider is expected to simply confirm." },
      { stem: "A query stating: 'Since the patient's creatinine rose to 2.1 from a baseline of 0.9 during the admission, please confirm the diagnosis of acute kidney injury' is problematic because:", options: ["It is fully compliant and requires no changes", "It suggests the specific diagnosis to be confirmed rather than presenting clinical indicators and asking an open, non-leading question about their clinical significance", "It uses too much clinical detail", "It should have been sent to a different department"], correct: 1, explanation: "Directly telling the provider what diagnosis to 'confirm' is a leading query — a compliant version would present the clinical indicators (creatinine trend, baseline) and ask the provider to clarify the clinical significance/diagnosis, if any, without presupposing the answer." },
      { stem: "Which of the following would generally be considered an appropriate, non-leading way to query for potential acute kidney injury based on the same creatinine trend?", options: ["'The patient's creatinine rose from a baseline of 0.9 to 2.1 during this admission. Please clarify if this represents acute kidney injury, a chronic process, another etiology, or is not clinically significant.'", "'Please diagnose the patient with acute kidney injury based on the creatinine values.'", "'Do you agree the patient has acute kidney injury? Yes/No.'", "'Please just add AKI to the problem list.'"], correct: 0, explanation: "This version presents the clinical indicator objectively and offers multiple genuine clinical possibilities (including 'not clinically significant'), letting the provider exercise independent clinical judgment — the hallmark of a compliant query." },
      { stem: "Identifying 'query opportunities' by analyzing current documentation primarily means:", options: ["Looking for any documentation gap regardless of whether it affects code assignment, severity, or compliance", "Recognizing clinical indicators, treatments, or test results in the record that are not matched by a specific enough diagnosis statement, an unaddressed conflict, or missing linkage needed for accurate and complete coding", "Querying every chart as a routine matter regardless of documentation quality", "Only querying when a physician requests it"], correct: 1, explanation: "Query opportunity identification is about spotting a genuine mismatch between clinical evidence and documented specificity/completeness that materially affects coding — not queries for their own sake or purely administrative gaps." },
      { stem: "A compliant query may appropriately include which of the following as an answer option?", options: ["Only the single diagnosis the coder or CDI specialist suspects is correct", "'Clinically undetermined' or 'unable to determine,' when genuinely clinically appropriate, alongside other reasonable options", "No answer options at all — an open free-text-only format is prohibited under all circumstances", "A pre-checked default answer that the provider must actively uncheck to disagree"], correct: 1, explanation: "Including options like 'clinically undetermined' (in addition to reasonable clinical alternatives) helps keep a multiple-choice-format query non-leading, since the provider is not forced into confirming a specific suspected diagnosis." },
      { stem: "Under a compliance-driven query program, verbal (non-written) queries are generally considered acceptable only when:", options: ["Never — all queries must always be written, with no exceptions under any organization's policy", "They are subsequently documented in the health record per organizational policy, since queries (verbal or written) should generally leave an auditable record of what was asked and answered", "They occur informally in a hallway conversation with no need for any record at all", "The coder simply remembers the conversation without documenting it"], correct: 1, explanation: "Regardless of format, sound query practice calls for the query and its resolution to be reflected/documented per organizational policy, preserving an auditable trail — an undocumented, purely verbal exchange undermines compliance and record completeness." },
      { stem: "Which of the following queries would most likely be considered leading and non-compliant?", options: ["'Based on the elevated white count, fever, and positive blood culture, please clarify whether the patient has sepsis, a localized infection, or another process.'", "'Please confirm the patient has sepsis so the correct DRG can be assigned.'", "'The chart notes possible pneumonia with no further documentation — can you clarify the final diagnosis?'", "'Please clarify whether the documented pressure ulcer was present on admission or developed during this stay.'"], correct: 1, explanation: "Explicitly tying the requested diagnosis to a reimbursement/DRG outcome, and asking the provider to simply 'confirm' a specific diagnosis, are both hallmarks of a leading, non-compliant query." },
      { stem: "A key reason ethical query compliance matters for CCS-credentialed coders specifically is:", options: ["Queries have no bearing on code accuracy or compliance risk", "Improperly leading queries can result in inaccurate code assignment, inflated reimbursement, and potential compliance/fraud exposure for the facility and the coding professional", "Query compliance is solely the CDI department's responsibility, never the coder's", "Only outpatient queries carry compliance risk"], correct: 1, explanation: "Because queries directly shape what gets documented and subsequently coded, leading queries create real compliance and fraud-exposure risk — this is why AHIMA's ethical coding standards and query-practice guidance treat non-leading query construction as a core coder competency." },
      { stem: "Analyzing documentation for query opportunities related to CC/MCC capture would most appropriately flag which of the following?", options: ["A stable, chronic condition clearly documented and consistently monitored with no new clinical significance during this stay", "Abnormal lab/vital sign trends (e.g., rising lactate, hypotension) suggestive of a clinically significant condition (e.g., sepsis, shock) that is not yet specifically diagnosed anywhere in the documentation", "A well-documented, fully specified principal diagnosis with clear supporting evidence", "Routine post-operative vital sign monitoring with no abnormal findings"], correct: 1, explanation: "A genuine query opportunity exists when objective clinical evidence suggests a condition of coding/severity significance that has not yet been specifically or consistently documented — not when documentation is already clear and complete." },
      { stem: "A retrospective query (submitted after discharge, before final billing) asking a provider to clarify a diagnosis based on clinical indicators already present in the discharge record is:", options: ["Never permitted under any circumstances", "Generally permissible under most organizational query policies, provided it remains non-leading and is based on clinical indicators already documented in the record — retrospective queries are common practice, subject to the same compliance standards as concurrent queries", "Only permitted if the diagnosis being asked about would lower reimbursement", "Automatically considered leading regardless of its wording"], correct: 1, explanation: "Retrospective (post-discharge, pre-bill) queries are a standard, permitted part of many query programs and must meet the same non-leading, indicator-based compliance standard as concurrent (during-stay) queries — timing alone doesn't make a query compliant or non-compliant." },
    ],
  },
  {
    name: "Domain 4 — Regulatory Compliance",
    sourceNote: SOURCE_NOTE_D4,
    questions: [
      { stem: "The HIPAA Privacy Rule primarily governs:", options: ["Technical safeguards for electronic systems only", "The use and disclosure of individually identifiable health information (protected health information, PHI) by covered entities and business associates", "Only paper-based medical records, not electronic records", "Only billing and claims data, not clinical documentation"], correct: 1, explanation: "The Privacy Rule addresses use/disclosure of PHI broadly (any form — paper, electronic, oral); the HIPAA Security Rule specifically addresses safeguards for electronic PHI (ePHI)." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best distinguishes the HIPAA Security Rule from the Privacy Rule?", options: ["The Security Rule addresses only paper records; the Privacy Rule addresses only electronic records", "The Security Rule establishes administrative, physical, and technical safeguards specifically for electronic PHI (ePHI); the Privacy Rule governs PHI use/disclosure in any form", "They are the same rule under different names", "The Security Rule applies only to health plans, never to providers"], correct: 1, explanation: "Security Rule = safeguards (administrative/physical/technical) for ePHI specifically; Privacy Rule = broader use/disclosure standards for PHI in any format." },
      { stem: "A Patient Safety Indicator (PSI) is best described as:", options: ["A billing code used only for outpatient claims", "A measure, often derived from administrative/claims data (including ICD-10-CM/PCS codes and POA indicators), used to screen for potentially preventable complications or adverse events occurring during hospitalization", "A type of provider license renewal requirement", "A patient satisfaction survey score"], correct: 1, explanation: "PSIs (an AHRQ Quality Indicator set) use coded administrative data — including POA indicators — to flag potentially preventable in-hospital complications, which is why accurate coding (especially POA) directly affects PSI measurement." },
      { stem: "A Hospital-Acquired Condition (HAC), for Medicare payment policy purposes, refers to a condition that:", options: ["Was present on admission and unrelated to hospital care", "Was not present on admission (POA = 'N') and meets CMS criteria as reasonably preventable through application of evidence-based guidelines, potentially affecting payment", "Is always fatal", "Only applies to conditions acquired during outpatient surgery"], correct: 1, explanation: "HACs are conditions not present on admission that CMS has identified as reasonably preventable — accurate POA indicator assignment is the coding mechanism that identifies whether a condition should be evaluated under the HAC payment provisions." },
      { stem: "Per AHIMA's Standards of Ethical Coding, a coder who is asked by a supervisor to assign a higher-weighted code without supporting clinical documentation should:", options: ["Comply, since supervisors have final authority over all coding decisions", "Decline to assign a code unsupported by documentation and escalate through appropriate compliance channels, since coding professionals are ethically obligated to code only what is supported by the health record", "Assign the code but tell no one", "Resign immediately without reporting the issue"], correct: 1, explanation: "AHIMA's ethical standards obligate coders to code strictly based on documented, supported clinical information — a request to inflate coding without support should be declined and escalated via compliance channels, not carried out." },
      { stem: "UHDDS compliance in the coding process most directly requires:", options: ["Following a facility's internal billing preferences over the standardized UHDDS data set definitions whenever there's a conflict", "Applying the standardized minimum data set definitions (e.g., principal diagnosis, other diagnoses, principal procedure) consistently, as UHDDS is the federally recognized uniform data set for inpatient discharge data reporting", "Reporting data only when convenient for the facility's case mix index", "Ignoring UHDDS for any non-Medicare patient"], correct: 1, explanation: "UHDDS defines a uniform minimum data set (principal diagnosis, additional diagnoses, principal/significant procedures, and more) that must be applied consistently for inpatient discharge reporting, regardless of payer, as the basis for standardized national data." },
      { stem: "Which of the following is an example of ensuring compliance with HIPAA's 'minimum necessary' standard in a coding department?", options: ["Coding staff having full, unrestricted access to every patient's entire longitudinal record across the health system regardless of job function", "Limiting coder access to only the PHI reasonably necessary to perform coding-related job functions for the specific encounters they are assigned", "Sharing PHI freely among all hospital departments regardless of need", "Applying 'minimum necessary' only to external requests, never to internal staff access"], correct: 1, explanation: "The 'minimum necessary' standard requires limiting PHI access/use/disclosure to what is reasonably necessary for the specific purpose — including internal staff access controls, not just external disclosures." },
      { stem: "A facility's HIM compliance program identifies a pattern of upcoding (assigning higher-severity diagnosis codes than clinical documentation supports) to increase reimbursement. This raises exposure under which federal law, among others?", options: ["The False Claims Act, which imposes liability for knowingly submitting false or fraudulent claims for payment to federal healthcare programs", "The Family and Medical Leave Act", "The Fair Labor Standards Act", "The Americans with Disabilities Act"], correct: 0, explanation: "Systematic upcoding that results in claims submitted to federal healthcare programs (Medicare/Medicaid) that don't match documented clinical reality creates False Claims Act exposure — a core reason coding accuracy and ethical query practice matter so much in this domain." },
      { stem: "Ensuring 'completeness and accuracy of health records' for regulatory compliance purposes includes verifying that:", options: ["Only the billing summary is complete; underlying clinical documentation completeness is irrelevant", "Required elements (authentication/signatures, timely documentation, consistent clinical detail supporting coded diagnoses/procedures) are present, consistent with both accreditation/regulatory standards and internal HIM policy", "Records are considered complete once discharged, regardless of missing signatures or dictation", "Completeness only matters for Medicare patients"], correct: 1, explanation: "Completeness/accuracy compliance spans authentication, timeliness, and the presence of documentation genuinely supporting all coded diagnoses and procedures — not just a superficially complete billing summary." },
      { stem: "Which best describes the general purpose of the HITECH Act as it relates to health information compliance?", options: ["It repealed the HIPAA Privacy and Security Rules entirely", "It strengthened HIPAA enforcement (including breach notification requirements) and promoted the adoption and meaningful use of electronic health records", "It applies only to paper-based health records", "It solely governs Medicare reimbursement rates"], correct: 1, explanation: "HITECH strengthened HIPAA privacy/security enforcement (notably introducing breach notification requirements) while also incentivizing EHR adoption and meaningful use — both aspects are relevant to CCS Domain 4/5 compliance knowledge." },
      { stem: "Under HIPAA's breach notification requirements (as strengthened by HITECH), a covered entity that experiences a breach of unsecured PHI is generally required to:", options: ["Notify affected individuals only if the breach involves more than 10,000 records", "Notify affected individuals (and, depending on breach size, HHS and potentially media) within specified timeframes following discovery of the breach", "Never notify anyone, to avoid reputational harm", "Wait until the next annual compliance audit to disclose the breach"], correct: 1, explanation: "Breach notification rules require notifying affected individuals (and HHS, with additional media notification for larger breaches) within defined timeframes after discovery — a compliance obligation coding/HIM staff should understand given their PHI access." },
      { stem: "A payer-specific coding/billing guideline conflicts with a general ICD-10-CM Official Guideline in a way that would result in a less accurate code being submitted to that payer. The generally correct compliance approach is:", options: ["Always follow the payer's guideline over the Official Guidelines, since payers control reimbursement", "Apply correct, compliant coding per the Official Guidelines as the baseline, and address payer-specific requirements (e.g., additional documentation, modifier use) through compliant means — not by assigning a code known to be clinically/technically inaccurate", "Refuse to bill that payer at all", "Assign both codes on the same claim regardless of payer edits"], correct: 1, explanation: "Payer-specific guidance can affect billing mechanics (documentation requirements, modifiers, claim edits) but does not override the obligation to code accurately per the Official Guidelines — knowingly submitting an inaccurate code to satisfy a payer preference is a compliance risk, not an accepted practice." },
      { stem: "The National Correct Coding Initiative (NCCI) edits are primarily designed to:", options: ["Increase reimbursement for facilities regardless of documentation", "Prevent improper payment for code pairs that should not be reported together for the same patient on the same date of service (e.g., unbundling a comprehensive procedure into its component parts), promoting correct coding and reducing improper Medicare payments", "Replace the need for ICD-10-CM diagnosis coding entirely", "Apply only to inpatient hospital claims"], correct: 1, explanation: "NCCI edits (Procedure-to-Procedure and Medically Unlikely Edits) target improper payment from unbundling and other incorrect code-pair combinations — a core regulatory-compliance concept tested under CCS Domain 1 (coding edits) and Domain 4 (regulatory compliance) alike." },
      { stem: "AHIMA's Standards of Ethical Coding specifically address a coder's obligation regarding query practices. Which best reflects that obligation?", options: ["Coders may construct queries however they wish, since ethical standards don't address query practice", "Coders should only query when a diagnosis affects reimbursement", "Coders are expected to query a provider for clarification when documentation is ambiguous, conflicting, or incomplete, rather than assigning a code based on assumption — consistent with the broader ethical duty to code accurately and completely based on the health record", "Coders should never query, deferring entirely to CDI staff"], correct: 2, explanation: "Ethical coding practice includes querying (compliantly, non-leading) when documentation genuinely doesn't support confident, accurate code assignment — assumption-based coding in place of a reasonable query is itself an ethics/compliance concern." },
      { stem: "A compliance program's internal auditing/monitoring function, as it relates to coding, primarily serves to:", options: ["Replace the need for coder training entirely", "Periodically review coded claims/records for accuracy and guideline adherence, identifying patterns of error (e.g., systemic upcoding, undercoding, or POA misassignment) before they result in larger compliance exposure", "Only occur once, at the time an employee is hired", "Apply exclusively to outpatient claims"], correct: 1, explanation: "Ongoing internal auditing/monitoring is a core element of an effective compliance program (per OIG compliance program guidance), catching systemic coding-accuracy issues proactively rather than only after external audit or payer recoupment." },
      { stem: "Under the False Claims Act, which of the following best describes 'knowingly' submitting a false claim, in the context most relevant to coding compliance?", options: ["Only literal, deliberate intent to defraud qualifies as 'knowing' — good-faith mistakes can never create liability under any circumstance", "'Knowingly' can include actual knowledge, deliberate ignorance, or reckless disregard of the truth or falsity of the claim — meaning a pattern of reckless, unverified coding practices can create exposure even without provable deliberate intent to defraud", "The False Claims Act applies only to physicians, never to coders or facilities", "Liability requires a signed confession from the coder involved"], correct: 1, explanation: "The False Claims Act's 'knowing' standard includes reckless disregard/deliberate ignorance, not just provable specific intent — this is why systemic, unverified coding shortcuts (not just deliberate fraud) carry real compliance risk." },
      { stem: "Which best describes the relationship between AHIMA's Standards of Ethical Coding and a facility's own internal coding compliance policy?", options: ["They are always identical documents with no distinction", "AHIMA's standards establish a professional ethical baseline expected of credentialed coders (like the CCS), while a facility's internal policy operationalizes compliance requirements specific to that organization — a coder is expected to meet both, and internal policy should not conflict with the ethical baseline", "Internal facility policy always overrides AHIMA's ethical standards when the two conflict", "AHIMA's standards apply only to AHIMA staff, not credential holders working at hospitals"], correct: 1, explanation: "Credentialed coders (CCS holders) are professionally bound by AHIMA's ethical standards regardless of employer, and a compliant facility's internal policy should operationalize — not conflict with — that professional ethical baseline." },
      { stem: "Which scenario best illustrates a HIPAA Security Rule administrative safeguard, as opposed to a physical or technical safeguard?", options: ["A locked server room door", "A firewall configuration on the hospital network", "A documented security risk assessment process and workforce security-training policy governing who may access ePHI systems and under what conditions", "An encrypted hard drive"], correct: 2, explanation: "Administrative safeguards are the policies/procedures/workforce management side of security (risk assessment, training, access-authorization policy); physical safeguards address facility/device physical access (locked doors); technical safeguards address the technology itself (encryption, firewalls, access controls in software)." },
      { stem: "A coding compliance plan's 'corrective action' component, following identification of a coding error pattern through internal audit, would most appropriately include:", options: ["No action, since errors are considered a normal and acceptable part of coding operations", "Targeted staff education/retraining on the specific identified error pattern, and, where required, correction/resubmission of affected claims consistent with applicable payer and regulatory requirements", "Termination of the entire coding department without further investigation", "Ignoring the pattern unless a payer specifically flags it first"], correct: 1, explanation: "Effective compliance programs respond to identified error patterns with targeted education and, where warranted, claim correction — proactive self-identification and correction is itself viewed favorably under OIG compliance guidance, versus ignoring a known pattern." },
      { stem: "Medical necessity coding edits are designed to verify that:", options: ["A procedure or service is supported by a diagnosis code that justifies it as reasonable and necessary under applicable payer coverage policy — mismatched diagnosis/procedure pairings can result in denied claims", "Every claim automatically qualifies for the highest available reimbursement", "Medical necessity applies only to inpatient claims", "Diagnosis codes are irrelevant to medical necessity determinations"], correct: 0, explanation: "Medical necessity edits check that the reported diagnosis code(s) support the reported service/procedure as reasonable and necessary per coverage policy (e.g., NCDs/LCDs for Medicare) — a mismatch is a common reason for denied claims, distinct from NCCI's code-pair-bundling focus." },
    ],
  },
  {
    name: "Domain 5 — Information Technologies",
    sourceNote: SOURCE_NOTE_D5,
    questions: [
      { stem: "An Electronic Health Record (EHR) is best distinguished from an Electronic Medical Record (EMR) by which characteristic?", options: ["An EHR is designed to be shared across and interoperable with other healthcare organizations, going beyond a single practice's internal digital chart (the traditional EMR concept)", "An EMR contains imaging data while an EHR never does", "There is no meaningful distinction between the two terms", "An EHR is always paper-based while an EMR is always electronic"], correct: 0, explanation: "EMR traditionally refers to a single organization's digital version of a paper chart; EHR is built around interoperability and information sharing across multiple organizations/providers involved in a patient's care." },
      { stem: "Encoder software in a coding department is primarily used to:", options: ["Replace the need for any coder judgment entirely, assigning all codes automatically with no human review", "Assist coders in identifying and sequencing correct codes through logic-based prompts, code lookups, and edits, while still relying on coder expertise and documentation review for accuracy", "Only generate patient billing statements", "Manage hospital staff scheduling"], correct: 1, explanation: "Encoders are a coding-support tool (logic-based code lookup, sequencing prompts, edit-checking) — they assist and structure the coding process but do not eliminate the need for coder clinical/coding judgment based on the actual documentation." },
      { stem: "Grouper software's primary function in an inpatient HIM/coding workflow is to:", options: ["Translate ICD-10-CM/PCS codes and other case data into the appropriate MS-DRG (or other DRG system) for reimbursement/severity classification purposes", "Convert paper records into electronic format", "Generate patient appointment reminders", "Perform real-time clinical decision support during a patient encounter"], correct: 0, explanation: "Grouper software applies DRG-assignment logic to coded diagnosis/procedure data (plus other case attributes like discharge disposition) to determine the appropriate MS-DRG or other grouping, which drives reimbursement and severity/quality reporting." },
      { stem: "Computer-Assisted Coding (CAC) software is best described as a tool that:", options: ["Fully replaces human coders with no oversight required", "Uses natural language processing (NLP) or structured data to suggest candidate codes from clinical documentation, which a credentialed coder then reviews, validates, and finalizes", "Is used exclusively for scheduling, not coding", "Automatically submits claims to payers with no human review step"], correct: 1, explanation: "CAC uses NLP/pattern recognition to surface suggested codes from documentation, but industry and regulatory expectations (and accreditation/compliance best practice) require a qualified coder to review and validate CAC-suggested codes before finalization — CAC assists, it does not replace, coder judgment." },
      { stem: "A known risk of over-reliance on CAC-suggested codes without adequate coder review is:", options: ["CAC software has no known risks or limitations", "CAC may suggest a code based on documentation language pattern-matching alone, without capturing full clinical context, POA status, or guideline nuance — risking inaccurate code assignment if not properly validated by a coder", "CAC always produces more accurate codes than a human coder in every scenario", "CAC cannot be used with ICD-10-CM/PCS at all"], correct: 1, explanation: "Because CAC often relies on NLP pattern-matching against documentation text, it can miss clinical nuance, sequencing rules, POA context, or guideline exceptions — human coder review remains essential for compliance and accuracy." },
      { stem: "Which of the following best reflects a HITECH-related compliance consideration specific to HIM information technology systems?", options: ["HITECH requirements are irrelevant to encoder/grouper/CAC systems", "Systems handling ePHI (including coding/abstracting systems) must operate consistent with HIPAA Security Rule safeguards as reinforced by HITECH, including appropriate access controls and audit trails", "HITECH only applies to insurance companies, never to hospital HIM departments", "HITECH eliminated the need for EHR security safeguards"], correct: 1, explanation: "Any system touching ePHI — including coding, abstracting, encoder, and CAC platforms — falls under HIPAA Security Rule obligations that HITECH reinforced (stronger enforcement, breach notification, meaningful-use-linked security requirements)." },
      { stem: "A 'legacy system' interoperability challenge in HIM technology most often refers to:", options: ["A brand-new EHR system with no historical data", "Difficulty exchanging or migrating data between an older health information system and newer platforms due to differing data standards, formats, or structures", "A system used only for scheduling appointments", "A system that has no electronic components at all"], correct: 1, explanation: "Legacy-system interoperability issues arise when older systems use data formats/structures incompatible with newer EHR/HIM platforms, complicating data exchange, migration, and coding workflows that span both." },
      { stem: "Which best describes the coder's continued responsibility when using an encoder or CAC tool that flags a potential code based on documentation?", options: ["Accept every system-suggested code without independent verification against the source documentation", "Independently verify that system-suggested codes are actually supported by, and consistent with, the documentation and applicable coding guidelines before finalizing them", "Disable all software assistance and code entirely without any technology tools", "Only verify codes that affect reimbursement, ignoring all others"], correct: 1, explanation: "Regardless of the technology tool used, the coder retains professional responsibility for verifying that any suggested code is actually supported by the documentation and correctly applies coding guidelines — technology assists, but does not transfer, that accountability." },
      { stem: "In the context of HIM technology, 'interoperability' most precisely means:", options: ["The ability of different information systems, devices, or applications to access, exchange, and cooperatively use data in a coordinated manner", "The physical durability of computer hardware", "A single vendor's product line compatibility with itself only", "The speed at which a single system processes a query"], correct: 0, explanation: "Interoperability is about the capacity for different systems/devices to exchange and meaningfully use data together — a foundational concept for EHR data exchange, health information exchanges (HIEs), and coding-adjacent system integration." },
      { stem: "Which scenario best illustrates a legitimate use of encoder/grouper software output as part of a compliant coding and DRG-assignment workflow?", options: ["A coder assigns codes based on documentation review and coding guidelines, uses the encoder to assist with code lookup/sequencing logic, and the grouper then calculates the resulting DRG from those finalized, documentation-supported codes", "A coder lets the grouper software choose which diagnoses to add to the record purely to reach a higher-weighted DRG, regardless of documentation", "A facility bypasses coder review entirely, submitting whatever DRG the grouper outputs from raw, unverified data", "The grouper software is used only after claims have already been submitted to payers"], correct: 0, explanation: "The compliant workflow is: documentation-driven coding (assisted, not replaced, by encoder logic) feeds into the grouper, which then calculates DRG from those verified codes — not the reverse (picking diagnoses to hit a target DRG, or skipping coder review of system output)." },
    ],
  },
];
